::: {.doc-subject}

Display the table in a notebook or a web browser.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/great-tables/blob/main/great_tables/_export.py#L70-L122){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
GT.show(target="auto")
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

Note that this function is often unnecessary in a notebook. However, it's sometimes useful for
manually triggering display within a code cell.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Literal[[&quot;auto&quot;]{.st}, [&quot;notebook&quot;]{.st}, [&quot;browser&quot;]{.st}]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;auto&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Where to show the table. If "auto", infer whether the table can be displayed inline (e.g. in
    a notebook), or whether a browser is needed (e.g. in a console).
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}

The example below when in the Great Tables documentation, should appear on the page.

::: {#1bf436c6 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from great_tables import GT, exibble

GT(exibble.head(2)).show()
GT(exibble.tail(2)).show()
```

